    question: "How long is Worldpay's notice period?",
    answer: '90 days written notice is typical on a standard Worldpay UK merchant services contract, served by recorded delivery to the registered Worldpay address before your renewal date. Verbal notice to a sales rep does not count, and email is grey — recorded delivery is the only route reliably evidenced if it later goes to dispute. Check your own contract for the exact wording, but plan around 90 days as the working assumption.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How is my early termination fee calculated?',
    answer: 'The ETF is months remaining on your initial term or current renewal multiplied by your monthly service fee plus monthly terminal rental, less any prepayment-discount adjustment. As a worked example: 18 months remaining at £35/month combined service + rental = £630 ETF. Use your latest statement (not the original sales quote) for the per-month figure, because fee creep means the statement number is usually higher.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I dispute the PCI non-compliance fee?',
    answer: "Yes, if you've completed your Self-Assessment Questionnaire on time and have evidence — typically the email confirmation from Worldpay's compliance partner (Sysnet or Security Metrics, depending on era) showing the SAQ submission date and result. Send the dates and reference numbers to Worldpay merchant services in writing and ask for a refund back to your most recent SAQ. Worldpay typically refunds the most recent 6–12 months without much pushback.",
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I leave Worldpay mid-contract without paying ETF?',
    answer: 'Only via material breach by Worldpay — for example, an unannounced rate increase that exceeds the contract\'s rate-change clause, prolonged settlement failures, or terminal failures Worldpay declined to remedy. Specific cases like these may give you a clean exit, but you need the breach documented in writing and ideally a solicitor\'s letter framing the termination. In ordinary cases the ETF stands and the cheaper play is to time notice to land near the renewal date.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What happens to the terminal?',
    answer: "If your terminal is rented (the most common case) you must return it per the contract — usually within 14 days of contract end via Worldpay's returns label, with photographic evidence of serial number and tracked posting. Some contracts include a buyout option, typically £100–£200, which lets you keep the device but means PCI obligations transfer to you and the terminal is locked to Worldpay's processing anyway, so it's rarely worth taking up.",
  },
  {
    question: 'How fast can I switch?',
    answer: 'Flat-rate readers are next-day to one-week. SumUp ships the Solo or Air with same-day to next-day onboarding once your KYC clears. Square is similar. Dojo onboards in 1–5 working days for most SMBs, faster for clean files. Stripe Terminal is dev-time on top, typically a week. The slower path is moving to another full merchant account (Teya, Tide Business, Take Payments) — that\'s 1–3 weeks.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Will Worldpay offer to reduce my fees to keep me?',
    answer: "Usually yes — it's normal practice for Worldpay's retention team to call once notice is logged, sometimes offering a meaningful rate reduction (10–30% off the blended rate) to keep the account. Negotiate hard if you'd otherwise stay; the rates they'll match are closer to the rest of the market than the original quote. Get any agreed reduction in writing as a contract amendment, not a verbal promise — verbal retention offers reliably evaporate within a billing cycle.",
  },
  {
    question: 'Does my new acquirer buy out my Worldpay contract?',
    answer: 'Some do. Dojo runs a buy-out scheme that contributes towards Worldpay ETF up to a published cap (the cap moves; ask before you sign). Take Payments and Paymentsense have offered similar contract-buyout incentives at various points. SumUp, Square and Zettle do not — flat-rate readers don\'t take on contractual liabilities. Always confirm the buyout in writing, including the cap and the evidence required (typically a final Worldpay invoice).',
