---
<BaseLayout
  title="Cancel Worldpay — UK Merchant Guide to Leaving Worldpay (2026)"
  description="How to cancel a Worldpay merchant services contract in the UK: notice period, early termination fee maths, the PCI non-compliance fee, and faster, cheaper alternatives."
  pathname="/switch/cancel-worldpay/"
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  ogType="article"
  publishedDate="2026-04-26"
  updatedDate="2026-04-26"
  author={{ name: 'Editorial team', url: '/about/' }}
>
  <Hero
    slot="hero"
    eyebrow="Switching › Cancel Worldpay"
    title="Cancel Worldpay — the notice mechanics, the ETF maths, and what to switch to."
    subtitle="Worldpay's contracts auto-renew on a rolling 12 months and want 90 days written notice. This is the exact playbook for getting out cleanly — and the maths on what you'd save by switching."
    lastUpdated="26 April 2026"
  />

  <ArticleSchema
    headline="Cancel Worldpay — UK guide to notice, ETF and switching"
    description="Worldpay contract structure, notice period, early termination fee calculations, PCI non-compliance fee disputes, and faster UK alternatives."
    datePublished="2026-04-26"
    dateModified="2026-04-26"
    authorName="Editorial team"
    authorUrl={`${SITE.url}/about/`}
    publisherName={SITE.name}
    publisherUrl={SITE.url}
    mainEntityOfPage={`${SITE.url}/switch/cancel-worldpay/`}
  />
  <FaqPageSchema faqs={faqs} />
  <HowToSchema
    name="How to cancel a Worldpay merchant services contract"
    description="The five-step process for serving notice on Worldpay, calculating any early termination fee, returning equipment, and confirming final billing."
    steps={howToSteps}
  />

  <section>
    <h2>What Worldpay's contract typically looks like</h2>
    <p>Worldpay's standard UK merchant services agreement is built around a fixed initial term — most commonly <strong>12, 18, 36 or 48 months</strong> — followed by an automatic rolling renewal. The initial term is the figure your sales rep quoted; the rolling renewal is where most merchants get caught out, because it's not the same thing as the original term restarting.</p>
    <p>The auto-renewal language in the current Worldpay terms is a <strong>rolling 12-month renewal</strong>: at the end of your initial term the contract renews for a further 12 months, and again every 12 months thereafter unless you serve notice in the correct window. That's why merchants who think they're "out of contract" three years in are often three months into a fresh 12-month commitment.</p>
    <p>The notice period attached to that renewal is typically <strong>90 days written notice</strong>, served before the renewal date. Verbal notice to a sales rep does not count. Email is grey — the contract usually specifies recorded delivery to the registered Worldpay address, and that's the only route that's reliably evidenced if it later goes to dispute.</p>
    <p>Two other clauses to check on your own paperwork before you do anything: the early termination fee (ETF) clause, which sets out the formula for any fee owed if you leave inside the term, and the equipment clause, which says whether the terminal is rented (must be returned) or bought outright (yours to keep, but PCI obligations transfer).</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How to calculate your early termination fee</h2>
    <p>The early termination fee on a Worldpay contract is built from three components: a <strong>terminal rental charge for the months remaining</strong>, a <strong>monthly service / minimum-monthly-fee charge for the months remaining</strong>, and a <strong>deduction for any prepayment discount</strong> you've already received. The formula isn't a flat exit penalty — it's a buy-out of the income Worldpay would have earned across the rest of your term.</p>
    <p>The arithmetic is straightforward once you have your latest statement to hand. Take the terminal rental line (commonly <strong>£25/month</strong> for a Worldpay-supplied countertop or portable), multiply by the number of full months remaining on your initial term or current renewal, and that's the rental component. Then take the monthly service or minimum-monthly fee (often £10–£15/month, sometimes bundled into a "service charge"), multiply by the same number of months remaining, and that's the service component.</p>
    <p><strong>Worked example.</strong> Assume your contract has 18 months remaining and your statement shows a £25/month terminal rental plus £10/month service fee — £35/month total. ETF base = 18 × £35 = <strong>£630</strong>. If you took a prepayment-discount sign-up incentive worth, say, £90, that may be added back on, taking the figure to £720. Worldpay will sometimes apply a small early-settlement discount (a few percent for paying the ETF in one lump) — ask, because it's not advertised.</p>
    <p>Your statement is the source of truth, not the original sales quote. Pull the last three months of statements before you call — the all-in monthly figure on the statement is what feeds the ETF, and it usually drifts higher than the original quote because of fee creep on scheme fees, authorisation fees and the PCI non-compliance fee (see below).</p>
    <div class="callout">Illustrative numbers. Your actual ETF depends on your specific contract, terminal model and any promotional discounts — always verify against your latest Worldpay statement and contract before serving notice.</div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The notice mechanics — how to actually leave</h2>
    <p>The five steps below are the playbook merchants who exit cleanly follow. Skip any of them and you risk an ETF claim, a residual PCI fee, or a disputed final invoice that drags on for months.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Request a written contract status from Worldpay.</strong> Email merchant services and ask for: your contract end date, current renewal status, notice period required, ETF formula, and equipment ownership status. Get it in writing — a phone-call answer is not evidence in a later dispute. This single document anchors every subsequent step.</li>
      <li><strong>Calculate your remaining term and ETF.</strong> Using the contract status letter and your last three monthly statements, work out months remaining and the ETF figure (see the maths above). If the ETF is large enough to wipe out a year of switching savings, time your notice to land near the renewal date so you exit at zero ETF rather than mid-term.</li>
      <li><strong>Send formal notice in writing by recorded delivery.</strong> Address it to the registered Worldpay UK office named in your contract (currently in London). Include your merchant ID, the company name on the contract, the date you intend the contract to end, and an explicit reference to the clause you're relying on. Recorded delivery gives you a tracking number and a signed-for receipt — both matter if Worldpay later claim non-receipt.</li>
      <li><strong>Confirm equipment return arrangements.</strong> If your terminal is rented (the most common case), Worldpay will issue a returns label and a deadline — typically 14 days from contract end. Photograph the device serial number and the sealed return parcel before posting, and keep the courier tracking. Failure to return triggers a non-return charge, often £200–£400.</li>
      <li><strong>Confirm final billing in writing.</strong> Two weeks after your contract end date, request a final statement and a written confirmation that no further charges will be raised. Cancel the direct debit only after this confirmation arrives — pulling the direct debit early gives Worldpay grounds to escalate to debt collection if any genuine final balance is outstanding.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>For the wording itself, see the <a href="/switch/notice-letter-template/">Worldpay notice-letter template</a> — copy, paste, adapt the dates and merchant ID, send recorded delivery.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The PCI non-compliance fee — when you owe it, when you don't</h2>
    <p>The PCI non-compliance fee is a monthly charge — typically <strong>around £35/month</strong> on a Worldpay merchant account, sometimes a little less, sometimes more — levied when Worldpay can't see evidence that you've completed your annual PCI DSS Self-Assessment Questionnaire (SAQ). It is one of the most common sources of fee creep on long-running Worldpay accounts, because the SAQ has to be re-validated every 12 months and easily lapses.</p>
    <p><strong>You owe the fee</strong> when your most recent SAQ has expired, when you've never completed one, or when the SAQ on file doesn't cover your actual processing setup (for example, you've moved from a standalone terminal to an integrated EPOS without re-validating). The fee accrues monthly until a fresh SAQ is logged with Worldpay's compliance partner.</p>
    <p><strong>You don't owe the fee</strong> if you've completed the SAQ on time, you have an Attestation of Compliance (AoC) dated within the last 12 months, and you can produce evidence — typically the email confirmation from Sysnet or Security Metrics (Worldpay's compliance providers, depending on era) showing the SAQ submission date and result. If those three things are true and the fee has still been charged, it's a billing error — not a contractual obligation.</p>
    <p>To dispute it: pull your AoC and the SAQ submission email, write to Worldpay merchant services with the dates and reference numbers, and ask for a refund of the fee back to the date your most recent SAQ was logged. Worldpay will usually refund the most recent 6–12 months without much pushback — older periods need persistence. See <a href="/switch/pci-fees-explained/">our PCI fees deep-dive</a> for the dispute letter wording and the typical timelines.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Faster, cheaper alternatives</h2>
    <p>Once you've decided to leave Worldpay, the four UK terminals below cover the vast majority of switch destinations. Each has next-day or near-next-day onboarding, transparent published rates, and no 36–48 month lock-in.</p>
    <ul class="alt-list">
      <li><a href="/reviews/dojo-go/">Dojo Go</a> — for hospitality and high-volume retail wanting next-day payouts, fast tipping flow, and a UK-built support team. Best fit if you currently process £5k+/month.</li>
      <li><a href="/reviews/sumup-solo/">SumUp Solo</a> — for sole traders, mobile traders and low-volume sellers. 1.69% flat, no monthly fee, optional printer dock. Best fit under £3k/month and where simplicity beats every other consideration.</li>
      <li><a href="/reviews/zettle-reader-2/">Zettle Reader 2</a> — for PayPal-aligned sellers, beauty and salon, and takeaway operators who want PayPal payouts and the Zettle EPOS app. 1.75% flat, no contract.</li>
      <li><a href="/reviews/stripe-reader-s700/">Stripe Reader S700</a> — for businesses already on Stripe online who want one settlement file across in-person and online. Fits SaaS-led, events, and ecommerce-with-popup operators.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Cross-reference: <a href="/switch/worldpay-alternatives/">the full Worldpay alternatives listicle</a> covers acquirer-grade options too (Dojo merchant, Teya, Tide Business, Take Payments) for merchants who want a full merchant account rather than a flat-rate reader.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Switching savings — the maths</h2>
    <p>The headline reason merchants switch off Worldpay is the all-in effective rate. Worldpay's blended cost on a typical SMB statement (interchange, scheme fees, authorisation fees, monthly service, PCI fee, terminal rental, all rolled in) commonly lands around <strong>2.0–2.3%</strong> of card turnover. Dojo's published rate for the same merchant size is <strong>0.79% + 5p per transaction</strong>; SumUp Solo and Zettle Reader 2 are flat-rate at <strong>1.69%</strong> and <strong>1.75%</strong> respectively.</p>
    <p><strong>Worked example.</strong> A merchant doing £5,000/month in card turnover across 200 transactions:</p>
    <table class="savings-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Provider</th><th>Calculation</th><th>Monthly cost</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Worldpay (blended ~2.1%)</td><td>£5,000 × 2.1%</td><td><strong>£105.00</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Dojo (0.79% + 5p × 200)</td><td>£5,000 × 0.79% + 200 × £0.05 = £39.50 + £10.00</td><td><strong>£49.50</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>SumUp Solo (1.69%)</td><td>£5,000 × 1.69%</td><td><strong>£84.50</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Zettle Reader 2 (1.75%)</td><td>£5,000 × 1.75%</td><td><strong>£87.50</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The Worldpay → Dojo move on this profile saves <strong>£55.50/month, or £666/year</strong>. Across a 36-month term that's roughly £2,000 — comfortably enough to absorb a mid-term ETF on most contracts and still come out ahead. SumUp / Zettle save less per month but win on zero monthly fee, no contract, and instant onboarding.</p>
    <div class="callout">These are illustrative numbers using published rates and a typical SMB profile. Your blended Worldpay rate sits on your actual statement — pull the last three months, divide total fees by total card turnover, and you have your real number to compare against. Try the <a href="/switch/exit-cost-calculator/">exit-cost calculator</a> for a contract-aware view.</div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>FAQs</h2>
    <Faq faqs={faqs} />
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  section { margin: 2rem 0; }
  section h2 { margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }
  section p { margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  .alt-list { list-style: none; padding: 0; }
  .alt-list li { margin: 0.5rem 0; padding: 0.75rem 1rem; border: 1px solid #e5e7eb; border-radius: 8px; }
  .alt-list li a { font-weight: 600; }
  .savings-table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 1rem 0; }
  .savings-table th, .savings-table td { border: 1px solid #e5e7eb; padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem; text-align: left; }
  .savings-table th { background: #f9fafb; }
  .callout {
    background: #fef3c7;
    border: 1px solid #f59e0b;
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 0.75rem 1rem;
    margin: 1rem 0;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }
</style>
